record akr.decision.completed-planning-work-satisfies-dependency/1 : decision {
    title "Completed planning work satisfies a dependency"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-core/src/validate/**",
        path "docs/02-data-model.md",
        path "docs/04-references-and-versioning.md",
        path "docs/05-validation-rules.md",
        path "docs/06-compiler-pipeline.md",
        path "docs/10-freshness-and-git.md",
        path "spec/**"
    ]
    decision """
        A completed planning record satisfies a depends_on prerequisite. Both pinned and floating depends_on references may therefore retain a completed target; abandonment, supersession, rejection, withdrawal, and disproof remain invalid dependency outcomes. Completion must preserve the edge as provenance instead of forcing a bookkeeping revision that deletes the milestone chain. Recorded as D-035.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.write-latency-and-lifecycle-suite ]
}

record akr.decision.compress-state-not-search/1 : decision {
    title "Compress the state, not the search: an advisor packet has two layers and one is withheld"
    state proposed
    scope [ all ]
    decision """
        Handing a task to a second model produces an advisor packet, not a summary. A packet carries two typed layers. Layer A is administrative fact: the user's request verbatim, HEAD and its subject, the ledger revision, dirty paths with digests, the session head, declared namespaces, the governing goal and records, the search envelope, commands with what they produced, baselines, constraints, evidence and artefacts. All of it may be compressed. Layer B is worker interpretation: hypotheses, what was examined, what was not examined, proposed approaches, searches already run. It may be recorded and may not be substituted for the problem. handoff open renders Layer A and reports only that Layer B exists; handoff reveal releases it and stamps the packet, so whether a review was independent is knowable afterwards. The task field is verbatim, the search envelope defaults to the whole project, and the workspace is fingerprinted by HEAD, the source-graph hash and a sha256 per dirty path so that open and verify report exact or drifted and name what moved. Packets live in .agent/handoffs/, gitignored, invisible to search, context and the compiler, and are never records. Recorded as D-040.
        """
    context """
        An agent doing the work reaches a point where a second model would help, and the obvious handoff is a summary of what it found. That destroys the reason for making it: if the advisor's value is finding what the first agent missed, a summary of what the first agent noticed makes it a perceptual bottleneck, and its blind spot silently becomes the advisor's boundary. Afterwards nobody can tell, because a narrowed task reads exactly like a narrow one. The opposite failure was measured too: an advisor arriving cold spends its most expensive tokens on questions with deterministic answers, and in one logged run had to discover and correct the base_rev requirement mid-task. Both failures are the same mistake in opposite directions.
        """
    consequences """
        .agent/ now has two ignored subtrees rather than the one D-036 settled on; that line of D-036 was reasoning about the .agents/ versus .agent/ collision, which a second subtree under the same name does not reintroduce. The MCP Tool::writes field widens from writes to .akr/records/ to changes the workspace, because readOnlyHint and --surface read both read it and both would be lying if a tool that created a packet reported itself read-only; a third runner, run_coordination, sits beside run_read and run_write. knowledge.handoff_open carries the largest budget on the surface, and because a packet is addressable its overflow path names knowledge.handoff_expand rather than truncating everything. There is deliberately no reveal-on-open argument over MCP where the command line has --reveal: the CLI flag is one process and one recorded act, an MCP flag would make a read-only tool write, and the one-implementation invariant still holds because akr handoff open reproduces knowledge.handoff_open exactly. AKR does not become a model orchestrator: it owns state, context, packet construction, retrieval, provenance and snapshot verification, and a packet is provider-neutral.
        """
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record akr.decision.dirty-source-graph-has-no-commit-provenance/1 : decision {
    title "Dirty source graphs do not claim HEAD provenance"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/session.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/store/**",
        path "docs/06-compiler-pipeline.md",
        path "docs/07-cli.md"
    ]
    decision """
        The source-graph hash is the exact identity of the AKR bytes a command read. The Git commit is attached only when those loaded AKR sources match the committed tree. A mid-flight build may still write rebuildable caches, the lock, and views from dirty AKR sources, but their commit provenance is absent rather than naming the pre-change HEAD. Freshness may still use HEAD as its history boundary; source provenance and history comparison are separate facts.
        """
}

record akr.decision.durable-evidence-artifact/1 : decision {
    title "A cited artefact must outlive the citation, and that is a rule rather than a flag check"
    state superseded
    scope [ all ]
    decision """
        An evidence record's artifact slot may not name a path under .agent/scratch. This is V-025, raising AKR-T023 at type-check over the model, not a guard on akr evidence add --artifact. Being a rule makes it true on all four routes that write an evidence record -- akr evidence add, akr evidence add-many --from, akr propose --kind evidence --from, and knowledge.propose over MCP -- and on any route added later. It is still a refusal at write time, because every write validates the resulting ledger before writing anything. The earlier AKR-C004 flag check is removed rather than kept alongside it: two implementations of one invariant is how they drift, and akr evidence add --artifact under scratch now exits 1 with a ledger diagnostic rather than 2 with a usage error, which is the more honest classification -- the record is wrong, not the command line. The comparison is textual and normalises separators, a leading ./ and an absolute path passing through a scratch directory, and it reads the artifact slot only, so a papercut quoting a scratch path in its statement is describing the problem rather than committing it. Recorded as D-037.
        """
    context """
        Scratch is the one directory the protocol documents as disposable, and akr scratch prune empties it on an ordinary handoff. An artefact cited from there is a verified claim whose backing a later session removes without knowing it was cited, and nothing notices, because a path is not a reference the ledger resolves. The audit behind the scratch-lifecycle work found 38 records across five files citing scratch paths, most already dangling. The first fix put the refusal in front of one flag; a review of the change found that three of the four write routes never reach that flag.
        """
    consequences """
        A ledger that already cites scratch artefacts fails akr check until those records are revised, including on the next unrelated write, because the write pipeline validates the whole resulting ledger. That is the intended forcing function and the same standing every other V-rule has, but it is a real migration cost for the workspaces the original audit was about; akr scratch keep <entry> is the escape hatch for an artefact that should stay where it is. This does not re-open the scratch-lifecycle ruling that scratch is never a ledger diagnostic: that is about the directory's contents, how much is sitting there and how old, which akr check reports as a build fact. V-025 is about a record, and the record is wrong whether or not the file behind it still exists.
        """
    derived_from [ @akr.papercut.collated-19-papercuts-from-evidence-intake/2 ]
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.v025-durable-artifact-suite/1 ]
}

record akr.decision.durable-evidence-artifact/2 : decision {
    title "A cited artefact must outlive the citation, and that is a rule rather than a flag check"
    state active
    scope [ all ]
    decision """
        An evidence record's artifact slot may not name a path under an unkept .agent/scratch entry. This is V-025, raising AKR-T023 at type-check over the model, not a guard on akr evidence add --artifact. The keep index is attached as a workspace fact on both read and write pipelines, so an entry protected by akr scratch keep is persistent and valid on all four evidence write routes; an unkept entry is refused before anything is written. The comparison is textual and normalises separators, a leading ./ and an absolute path passing through a scratch directory, and it reads the artifact slot only. Recorded as D-037 and clarified after field verification of the documented keep escape hatch.
        """
    context """
        Scratch is the one directory the protocol documents as disposable, and akr scratch prune empties it on an ordinary handoff. An artefact cited from there is a verified claim whose backing a later session removes without knowing it was cited, and nothing notices, because a path is not a reference the ledger resolves. The audit behind the scratch-lifecycle work found 38 records across five files citing scratch paths, most already dangling. The first fix put the refusal in front of one flag; a review of the change found that three of the four write routes never reach that flag.
        """
    consequences """
        A ledger that already cites scratch artefacts fails akr check until those records are revised, including on the next unrelated write, because the write pipeline validates the whole resulting ledger. That is the intended forcing function and the same standing every other V-rule has, but it is a real migration cost for the workspaces the original audit was about; akr scratch keep <entry> is the escape hatch for an artefact that should stay where it is. This does not re-open the scratch-lifecycle ruling that scratch is never a ledger diagnostic: that is about the directory's contents, how much is sitting there and how old, which akr check reports as a build fact. V-025 is about a record, and the record is wrong whether or not the file behind it still exists.
        """
    derived_from [
        @akr.papercut.collated-10-papercuts-from-evidence-intake-2/1,
        @akr.papercut.collated-19-papercuts-from-evidence-intake/2
    ]
    supersedes [ @akr.decision.durable-evidence-artifact/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.collated-papercut-reconciliation-2026-08-23/1,
        @akr.evidence.v025-durable-artifact-suite/1
    ]
}

record akr.decision.executable-authoring-contracts/1 : decision {
    title "Authoring and transport contracts are executable"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/freshness/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/validate/**",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/**",
        path "docs/**",
        path "scripts/setup-akr-mcp.*",
        path "spec/**"
    ]
    decision """
        AKR authoring surfaces must expose typed per-kind slots, legal relations, canonical
        reference forms, and an executable recovery step before or with a rejected write.
        Scope globs matching no tracked path are non-fatal authoring warnings. Successful MCP
        writes name the required build step. MCP reliability is verified through a long-lived
        installed subprocess running mixed successful and rejected calls, while setup verifies
        the installed binary and tells clients to reconnect after replacement.
        """
    context """
        Repeated papercuts were caused less by missing capability than by contracts that became
        visible only after a failed call, plus transport reports not covered by in-memory tests.
        """
    consequences """
        Help, schemas, diagnostics, and write responses duplicate a small amount of generated
        vocabulary for discoverability. Transport coverage costs additional test time but
        exercises the connector boundary users actually run.
        """
    derived_from [ @akr.papercut.collated-18-papercuts-from-evidence-intake/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.mcp-sister-transport-reproduction/1,
        @akr.evidence.papercut-reconciliation-suite/1
    ]
}

record akr.decision.executable-authoring-contracts/2 : decision {
    title "Authoring and transport contracts are executable"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/**",
