record akr.decision.external-sources-immutable-library/1 : decision {
    title "External sources are an immutable library, indexed separately, cited by byte range"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/source.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/source/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/store/sources.rs",
        path "docs/15-external-sources.md",
        path "sources/**",
        path "spec/schema/sources.sql"
    ]
    decision """
        Outside advice is held in three layers with one responsibility each. The immutable
        source library, `sources/`, holds exact outside bytes: `akr source add` content-hashes
        a document, copies it under `sources/external/`, and adds a catalog entry, creating no
        records; editing a registered file is AKR-S021 from both `akr source verify` and
        `akr check`, and the only correction is registering a superseding version. The derived
        source index, `.akr/cache/sources.sqlite`, holds rebuildable, non-authoritative semantic
        chunks — heading paths, byte and line ranges, normalised search text, expanded technical
        symbols, ranked by BM25 — on its own generation, `source_corpus_hash`, distinct from the
        ledger's `source_graph_hash`, so a record write never rechunks the corpus and a
        registration never re-resolves the ledger. The ledger holds only the project's
        interpretation: records cite the library through a `source` block naming `document` plus
        `start_byte`, `end_byte`, `start_line`, `end_line` and an optional `excerpt_hash` —
        all-or-nothing, checked by `akr check` (AKR-S022) — never by chunk id, because chunk
        boundaries are allowed to move and provenance is not.
        """
    context """
        A first attempt at ingesting an outside advisor report fragmented it into one proposed
        record per heading, kept only the first paragraph of each, and let the original be
        deleted: the detail was gone, the fragments were never reviewed, the graph did not
        connect them, and the work board filled with unadopted headings. Markdown is the right
        format for the first reading of a rich technical audit; the question was what AKR should
        hold instead, and how a record should point at the part of a report it was written about.
        """
    consequences """
        Sparse adoption is the default: a record exists only because the project adopted,
        rejected, deferred or decided to track something in the material, and everything else
        stays readable in the report rather than becoming unreviewed project state.
        """
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.p10-chunk-unit-suite/1,
        @akr.evidence.p10-source-library-suite/1
    ]
}

record akr.decision.external-sources-immutable-library/2 : decision {
    title "External sources are immutable while registered and explicitly finalizable"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/source.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/source/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/store/sources.rs",
        path "docs/15-external-sources.md",
        path "sources/**",
        path "spec/schema/sources.sql"
    ]
    decision """
        Outside advice is held in three layers with one responsibility each. The source library is non-authoritative and content-hashed: registered bytes are immutable while present, but registration is not permanent. `akr source add` copies a document under `sources/external/` and creates no records. `akr source finalize` can transition a document from `full` to `cited-only`, retaining exact cited ranges in shared content-addressed `.akr/source-fragments/` blobs, or to `metadata-only` when no exact citations remain. The catalog tombstone retains title, origin, identity and the full document hash; the original Markdown may leave the active source tree.

        The derived source index, `.akr/cache/sources.sqlite`, holds rebuildable, non-authoritative semantic chunks on its own generation, distinct from the ledger's `source_graph_hash`. Full sources are searchable; finalized fragments are explicit provenance, not ordinary project search results. The ledger holds the project's interpretation: records cite a document and exact byte range, optionally with a role and `use` note explaining what the project adopted. Both CLI authoring and `knowledge.propose`/`knowledge.revise` must accept that exact locator; registration without an explicit adoption path is not a plan import. A record must remain semantically self-contained when its provenance blocks are hidden. `akr check` resolves exact citations from the full document or retained fragments and refuses a citation whose bytes are unavailable. Lineage-only references are valid in `metadata-only` state.

        Finalization does not rewrite records, duplicate excerpts into them, change a work state, satisfy evidence, or imply that outside advice was implemented. It is a source retention transition, separate from provenance interpretation and work completion.
        """
    context """
        The first source-library design made registered Markdown permanently undeletable in
        order to preserve exact citations. Use across multiple projects showed that advisor
        reports are often temporary intake documents, while the exact passages that records
        cite still need portable provenance. Literal absorption into every record would create
        revision noise and duplicate overlapping prose, so shared retained fragments preserve
        exact resolution without making ordinary context packets larger.
        """
    consequences """
        Source cleanup is explicit and inspectable through `source status`, `source dependents`
        and `source finalize --dry-run`. Full documents remain appropriate for standards and
        references that may yield future records; cited-only is the normal final state for
        advisor reports; metadata-only is permitted only for lineage references. A missing full
        source is an error, a missing retained fragment is an error, and a metadata-only exact
        citation is an error. Git history may retain the removed Markdown, but portable AKR
        fragments do not depend on that history.
        """
    supersedes [ @akr.decision.external-sources-immutable-library/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.p10-chunk-unit-suite/1,
        @akr.evidence.p10-source-library-suite/1
    ]
}

record akr.decision.git-facts-batched-by-ledger-file/1 : decision {
    title "Git facts are batched by ledger file"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/session.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/git/**",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/src/tools.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        Git remains the snapshot and acceptance-provenance boundary, but knowledge writes must not derive one record at a time by spawning Git history queries for every revision. Compute definitional last-change facts in batches by ledger file, parse each historical file version once, and avoid a second Git-enriched session open merely to format a successful write response. Lifecycle state, notes, verified_by links, unrelated records in the same file, and commits that only alter those bookkeeping fields do not move a record's definitional last change. Recorded as D-036.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.write-latency-and-lifecycle-suite ]
}

record akr.decision.mcp-budgeted-and-instrumented/1 : decision {
    title "The MCP surface is budgeted and instrumented"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/akr-mcp/**" ]
    decision """
        knowledge.get takes summary|body|canonical, defaulting to body. Every MCP result is
        measured across both halves and one over its hard limit is withheld rather than
        shortened, with its counts and a ready-made narrower call. akr-mcp --surface read omits
        the write tools; --accounting appends one JSON line per call. The server publishes its
        vocabulary version and reports a workspace disagreement as a named skew. Recorded as
        D-034.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.mcp-budget-and-skew-suite ]
}

record akr.decision.mcp-budgeted-and-instrumented/2 : decision {
    title "The MCP surface is budgeted and instrumented"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/akr-mcp/**" ]
    decision """
        knowledge.get takes summary|body|canonical, defaulting to body. MCP results without a caller-selected output budget are measured across both text and structured halves and one over its per-tool hard limit is withheld rather than shortened, with its counts and a ready-made narrower call. knowledge.context is different: when budget_tokens is explicit, context assembly owns that budget and the MCP adapter must not discard the assembled bundle behind a smaller fixed ceiling. knowledge.validate pages diagnostics by limit and offset, defaults to a bounded first page, and reports the total and next offset so a required handoff validation cannot be withheld without an actionable continuation. knowledge.evidence_add_many accepts 1–100 ordinary evidence payloads and commits all of them through one atomic validation pass or none; single writes and bulk writes reload only the written workspace state for their response rather than reopening a Git-enriched session. Authoring schemas state that topics are single segments and distinguish depends_on prerequisites, including completed planning prerequisites, from derived_from provenance. akr-mcp --surface read omits the write tools; --accounting appends one JSON line per call. The server publishes its vocabulary version and reports a workspace disagreement as a named skew. Recorded as D-034.
        """
    supersedes [ @akr.decision.mcp-budgeted-and-instrumented/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.collated-papercut-fixes,
        @akr.evidence.mcp-budget-and-skew-suite
    ]
}

record akr.decision.mcp-budgeted-and-instrumented/3 : decision {
    title "The MCP surface is budgeted and instrumented"
    state active
    scope [ path "crates/akr-mcp/**" ]
    decision """
        knowledge.get takes summary|body|canonical, defaulting to body. MCP results without a caller-selected output budget are measured across both text and structured halves. A result over its per-tool hard limit must still return a useful first page or compact preview and an actionable continuation that preserves every required argument; wholesale replacement with a truncation notice is forbidden. knowledge.start and knowledge.context are internally budgeted: when budget_tokens is explicit, context or handoff assembly owns that budget and the MCP adapter must not discard the assembled result behind a smaller fixed ceiling. Search continuations page deterministically through ranked results rather than merely retrying a smaller first page. knowledge.validate pages diagnostics by limit and offset, defaults to a bounded first page, and reports the total and next offset. knowledge.evidence_add_many accepts 1–100 ordinary evidence payloads and commits all of them through one atomic validation pass or none; single writes and bulk writes reload only the written workspace state for their response rather than reopening a Git-enriched session. Authoring schemas state that topics are single segments and distinguish depends_on prerequisites, including completed planning prerequisites, from derived_from provenance. akr-mcp --surface read omits the write tools; --accounting appends one JSON line per call. The server publishes its vocabulary version and reports a workspace disagreement as a named skew. Recorded as D-034 and revised after collated field evidence showed wholesale withholding made the tools unusable.
        """
    supersedes [ @akr.decision.mcp-budgeted-and-instrumented/2 ]
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.collated-papercut-fixes,
        @akr.evidence.mcp-budget-and-skew-suite
    ]
}

record akr.decision.mcp-envelope-verified-against-real-clients/1 : decision {
    title "The MCP envelope is verified against real client generations, not against our own reading"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-mcp/src/protocol.rs",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/tests/conformance.rs",
        path "docs/08-mcp.md",
        path "scripts/verify-mcp-conformance.sh",
        path "tools/mcp-conformance/**"
    ]
    decision """
        The MCP wire envelope is verified in two layers, and neither subsumes the other.
        `crates/akr-mcp/tests/conformance.rs` checks every response against closed sets written
        out by hand, with no dependency of any kind; `tools/mcp-conformance/` drives the server
        with three major versions of the rmcp client library at once, from outside the
        workspace, run by `scripts/verify-mcp-conformance.sh` and by CI. The first layer is the
        only one that catches an omission -- a supported protocol version quietly missing is not
        a malformed response and no client will ever report it. The second is the only one that
        catches a client generation becoming stricter than the one before it, which is how every
        defect here has actually been found. A published JSON Schema is not a third option that
        replaces either: the 2026-07-28 schema types `resultType` as an unconstrained string, so
        it admits the exact value that broke every tool call.
        """
    context """
        Three defects shipped through the same gap: `supportedProtocols` on a discovery result,
        a discovery result shaped like an initialize result, and `resultType: "tool"` on every
        tool this server has. None produced a JSON-RPC error. Each was a successful response a
        strict client could not deserialise, which is worse than a refusal, because a client
        retreats from an error and can only give up on a malformed success.

        They were invisible for a fortnight for a reason worth recording: nothing in this server
        regressed. Claude Code made 586 successful tool calls between 4 and 17 August, and Codex
        0.147 called eight knowledge tools on 13 August, against a server already emitting
        `resultType: "tool"` -- rmcp 1.7 has no concept of the field and serde dropped it. Codex
        0.149 bundles rmcp 3.1.2, which types results by it, and every tool call stopped working
        on 21 August with the server unchanged. The threat is client strictness drifting under a
        server that never moved, and rmcp alone has published 62 versions across four major
        lines.
        """
    consequences """
        `tools/mcp-conformance` is excluded from the workspace and keeps its own lock file, so
        `cargo test` is unchanged and `akr-mcp` keeps its zero dependencies, dev-dependencies
        included. The cost is a second Cargo project and about 150 crates that CI builds and no
        shipped binary links. Older client generations are kept in the matrix deliberately as
        the control: a defect that fails on 3.x and passes on 1.x has reproduced the real event
        rather than merely failing.
        """
}

record akr.decision.one-writer-at-a-time/1 : decision {
    title "The write pipeline claims the workspace, and refuses a source that moved under it"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "crates/akr-core/src/ops/**", path "docs/07-cli.md" ]
    decision """
        Step 0 of the write pipeline takes an advisory lock on `.akr/cache/write.lock` through the operating system's own file locking, held from before the ledger is read until every touched file has been renamed into place. Writers therefore queue and all of them succeed. Immediately before the renames, every file about to be replaced is re-read and must still hold the bytes step 1 read; a disagreement is `AKR-C034` and writes nothing.
        """
    context """
        The pipeline is a read-modify-write and only its renames were atomic, so two processes that both read before either wrote each held a ledger that did not know about the other's record, and the second rename discarded the first while both reported success. Six concurrent `akr papercut` processes landed two records (@akr.observation.concurrent-writes-are-silently-lost). Several agent hosts against one workspace is the normal way this ledger is used. `base_rev` did not cover it: that is optimistic concurrency over one key's head revision, and a newly proposed key engages it nowhere.
        """
    consequences """
        Two mechanisms rather than one, because a lock binds only the processes that take it. The lock lives on an open file handle, so the kernel releases it when a process ends however it ends — no stale lock to reap, no timeout to tune — and it costs no dependency, `std::fs::File::lock` being stable. The re-read is the backstop for what the lock cannot cover: an editor, a `git checkout`, or a filesystem whose locking does not work, where the guarantee degrades from `writers queue` to `a racing writer is told` rather than back to silent loss. The critical section holds no git and no index work, so a queue of agents moves through it in milliseconds each. Measured: sixteen concurrent proposals of distinct keys, sixteen landed; with the lock disabled, three landed and the other thirteen were refused rather than lost.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.observation.concurrent-writes-are-silently-lost ]
}

record akr.decision.papercut-subject-and-collation/1 : decision {
    title "A papercut says what it was about"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-core/src/papercut/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/render/papercuts.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        A papercut gains an optional `about` slot naming what the friction was with, when that
        is not this project. `akr papercut collate --about <subject>` gathers only the sisters'
        papercuts aimed at one subject, and counts what the filter left behind rather than
        dropping it silently. A collation carries each source papercut's full statement, not a
        truncated title; PAPERCUTS.md renders a collation as one summary line and gives
        subject-bearing papercuts their own heading. Recorded as D-033.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.papercut-subject-suite ]
}

record akr.decision.papercut-subject-and-collation/2 : decision {
    title "A papercut says what it was about"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/write.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/papercut/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/render/papercuts.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        A papercut has an optional `about` slot naming what the friction was with when that is not its owning project. By default `akr papercut collate` gathers only sister papercuts whose `about` matches the namespace receiving the master record; `--about <subject>` selects another subject, and `--all` explicitly takes every subject. The command counts filtered records rather than dropping them silently, carries every selected statement in full, and names only contributing projects in the master title. PAPERCUTS.md renders a collation as one summary line and gives subject-bearing papercuts their own heading. Recorded as D-033.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.collated-papercut-fixes ]
}

record akr.decision.papercut-subject-and-collation/3 : decision {
    title "A papercut says what it was about"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/write.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/papercut/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/render/papercuts.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        A papercut has an optional about slot naming what the friction was with when that is not its owning project. By default akr papercut collate gathers only sister papercuts whose about matches the namespace receiving the master record; --about selects other subjects and --all explicitly takes every subject. The command counts filtered records, carries every selected statement in full, and names only contributing projects. Every historical collated slot contributes to the dedup set even after its master becomes terminal: resolving or withdrawing a master never makes the absorbed source reports new again. PAPERCUTS.md renders a collation as one summary line and gives subject-bearing papercuts their own heading. Recorded as D-033 and revised after terminal masters replayed resolved reports.
        """
    derived_from [ @akr.papercut.collated-10-papercuts-from-evidence-intake-2/1 ]
    supersedes [ @akr.decision.papercut-subject-and-collation/2 ]
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.collated-papercut-fixes,
        @akr.evidence.collated-papercut-reconciliation-2026-08-23/1
    ]
}

record akr.decision.post-commit-validation/1 : decision {
    title "Post-commit identity is graph-based and co-committed evidence is current"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-core/src/render/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/resolve/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/validate/**",
        path "docs/16-change-protocol.md"
    ]
    topic post-commit-validation
    decision """
        Generated views embed the source-graph hash but no Git commit hash. A commit hash
        cannot be embedded in bytes that participate in that same commit; Git maps the stable
        graph to snapshots through AKR-Graph trailers and knowledge.start reports HEAD.

        Passing evidence is current when it descends from the verified record's last content
        change, or when the evidence record and verified record were introduced or changed in
        the same commit. Co-commit identity proves the evidence was authored for the prepared
        snapshot without weakening rejection of genuinely older evidence.
        """
    context """
        The previous banner made every clean post-commit build differ only by the unknowable
        future commit hash. Evidence observed against the prepared parent similarly became
        stale when the implementation and its completion landed together.
        """
    consequences """
        Generated projections are stable across the commit boundary and remain traceable by
        source graph. Acceptance freshness preserves D-016 while explicitly supporting the
        staged-tree workflow documented by the change protocol.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.post-commit-validation-suite/1 ]
}

record akr.decision.query-words-dirty-tree-ancestry/1 : decision {
    title "A query is words; a dirty tree is a fact"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/session.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/git/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/store/search.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        akr search escapes its query into quoted terms by default, with raw FTS5 behind --fts
        and never over MCP. AKR-G004 is exempt from the strict promotion, as staleness is
        under D-024. ancestry_over uses one cat-file --batch-check and one rev-list
        --topo-order instead of a comparison sort whose comparator forked git. Recorded as
        D-035.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.sister-papercut-fixes ]
}

record akr.decision.query-words-dirty-tree-ancestry/2 : decision {
    title "A query is words; a dirty tree is a fact"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/session.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/git/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/store/search.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        akr search escapes ordinary query terms and joins them with OR by default, with BM25 ranking records that match more terms first; raw FTS5 remains behind --fts and is never exposed over MCP. This makes multi-word natural-language orientation recall useful records even when no single record repeats every task word. AKR-G004 is exempt from strict promotion under D-024. ancestry_over uses one cat-file --batch-check and one rev-list --topo-order instead of a comparison sort whose comparator forked git. Recorded as D-035 and revised after field reports of silent multi-word misses.
        """
    derived_from [ @akr.papercut.collated-10-papercuts-from-evidence-intake-2/1 ]
    supersedes [ @akr.decision.query-words-dirty-tree-ancestry/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.collated-papercut-reconciliation-2026-08-23/1,
        @akr.evidence.sister-papercut-fixes
    ]
}

record akr.decision.revision-and-replacement-contract/1 : decision {
    title "Revision and replacement contracts are explicit"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/write.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/context/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/ops/**",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/src/tools.rs",
        path "docs/04-references-and-versioning.md",
        path "docs/07-cli.md",
        path "docs/08-mcp.md",
        path "docs/09-context-assembly.md"
    ]
    decision """
        An explicit lifecycle state on a revision is authoritative and lands on the successor.
        A content-only revision of sealed knowledge starts proposed so changed content is
        accepted deliberately. Context accepts bare keys, @keys, and a pin to the current head;
        it rejects historical pins and anchors with retrieval guidance. Replacing a different
        key is a two-step contract: first propose the complete same-kind replacement, then
        atomically attach its pinned supersedes edge and retire the old head.
        """
    context """
        Reconciled papercuts showed that CLI and MCP advertised overlapping reference and
        replacement forms but applied different lifecycle defaults and recovery paths.
        """
    consequences """
        Callers can predict successor state and reference handling. Different-key replacement
        requires one extra authoring call, but the final graph transition remains atomic and
        never constructs an incomplete replacement implicitly.
        """
    derived_from [ @akr.papercut.collated-18-papercuts-from-evidence-intake/1 ]
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.papercut-reconciliation-suite/1 ]
}

record akr.decision.revision-is-not-redefinition/1 : decision {
    title "Revising a record is not redefining it"
    state active
    scope [ path "crates/akr-core/**", path "docs/**" ]
    topic freshness-definitional-change
    decision """
        Revising a record is not redefining it. `last_change` compares definitions across the
        revision boundary: the projection it hashes drops the revision number and a
        `supersedes` naming an earlier revision of the same key, on top of everything D-029
        already removes, and the history walk continues past the commit that introduced a
        revision into the history of the revision it was revised from. A `supersedes` naming
        a different key is a real editorial statement and is untouched, and every definitional
        change D-029 keeps still moves the gate.
        """
    context """
        D-029 narrowed "content change" to what a record requires, so completing a milestone
        would not strand the evidence that closes it. That reaches only the in-place case, and
        D-015 makes a sealed record uneditable — revising it is the only way to change it.
        Revision n+1 did not exist before the commit that wrote it, so its last definitional
        change was that commit by construction and every citation it carried was instantly too
        old. Retargeting a check at a measurement that had already landed un-satisfied the
        check, with no way to re-land it: a fresh observed_at would have to name a commit that
        does not exist yet, and duplicating the evidence duplicates a measurement nobody re-ran.
        """
    consequences """
        A revision that changes nothing definitional preserves its predecessor's last_change,
        so acceptance verdicts survive a lifecycle edit, a note, and a plain retarget. This can
        lower an existing last_change, so a ledger built before and after this change can
        disagree about which commit that was; it is a projection difference, not a diagnostic.
        Byte reproducibility is unaffected — the computation stays a pure function of the
        repository and the ledger. The D-015 seal still hashes the whole revision.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.papercuts-2026-08-25/1 ]
}

record akr.decision.search-refreshes-disposable-index/1 : decision {
    title "Search refreshes its disposable index before querying"
    state active
    scope [
        path ".akr/cache/index.sqlite",
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/commands.rs",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/**"
    ]
    topic record-search-cache-refresh
    decision """
        akr search and knowledge.search must rebuild a missing or source-graph-stale record index from the currently loaded ledger before querying it. This cache refresh may occur as a side effect of a read because the cache is disposable and non-authoritative; it must not modify records, the lock, or generated views. When --no-rebuild is set, a needed refresh fails with AKR-I031 and leaves the cache untouched.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.search-stale-warning/1 ]
}

record akr.decision.start-collates-session-head/1 : decision {
    title "Start collates a validated session head"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/**",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/**",
        path "docs/**"
    ]
    topic session-head-briefing
    decision """
        AKR start and knowledge.start automatically prepend a deterministic, bounded project
        handoff assembled from the latest validated ledger snapshot, reachable AKR-linked Git
        history, live planning heads, review state, and any valid working-tree ledger overlay.
        Task-specific search remains in the same response, and canonical authority remains in
        records and Git rather than the projection.
        """
    context """
        Agents currently spend the beginning of sessions searching for whichever planning
        record is newest. A collated session head should provide the recent work focus and all
        outstanding branches before deeper task-specific retrieval.
        """
    consequences """
        Start becomes Git-backed while search remains ledger-only. The handoff is an ephemeral
        projection, not a new record kind or committed generated file.
        """
    supported_by [ @akr.evidence.session-head-briefing-suite/1 ]
}

record akr.decision.write-answers-for-what-it-introduces/1 : decision {
    title "A write answers for what it introduces, not for what it inherits"
    state active
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-core/src/git/mod.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/ops/mod.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/validate/rules.rs",
        path "docs/07-cli.md"
    ]
    topic write-refusal-scope
    decision """
        Step 3 of the write pipeline compares rather than asserts. It derives the resulting ledger's diagnostics and, only when there are any, restores the pre-edit text of every touched file, re-derives, and takes the difference as a multiset keyed on code, rule, subject and message, never on span. A diagnostic this write introduced refuses it, nothing written and the working tree byte-identical. One the ledger already had does not, and is reported as a note naming the count and the codes.
        """
    context """
        "Validates the resulting ledger" had been implemented as "the resulting ledger has no errors", which refuses every write into a ledger that is already invalid — including the write that would repair it, since each repair must first produce a ledger with none of the other faults in it. V-025 arrived on 2026-08-23 and judges history, so raw-autotune was left carrying 35 unfixable AKR-T023s and Lege-ecosystem 20 more plus 8 AKR-R022s, with hand-editing a .akr file the only escape and the protocol forbidding it. An agent hit it and wrote its findings into a Markdown file instead, which is the outcome the ledger exists to prevent. A promise that cannot survive its own violation is a latch, not an invariant; the one worth keeping is that a write never makes the ledger worse.
        """
    consequences """
        A broken ledger is repairable one record at a time through the sanctioned path. The intake guarantee is unchanged: evidence is born verified at revision 1, so a citation made today is always a new subject, introduced, and refused on all four write routes. A write can now land into a ledger that does not build, and akr build, akr validate and akr check are unmoved and still fail on the whole ledger, so nothing here hides drift. Two extra derivations are paid only on the path that was about to fail anyway. Two diagnostics were reworded alongside it: AKR-T023's help now names dropping the artifact slot, the only repair left once scratch has been pruned, and AKR-R022 distinguishes evidence that genuinely predates a record's last definitional change from evidence observed on a branch this history does not contain — the ancestry fact being a topological order that cannot tell the two apart, so a separate reachability fact answers it. That moves wording only; across all thirteen AKR workspaces the diagnostic profile is byte-identical before and after.
        """
    supported_by [
        @akr.evidence.repairable-ledger-field-proof/1,
        @akr.evidence.repairable-ledger-write-pipeline/1
    ]
}
